/* ---------- UNDERTOW: the machinery under the price ----------
   v: 'V' confirmed against a primary or reputable source fetched 4 Oct 2026; 'S' reputable secondary source; 'U' not confirmed (shown as such).
   st: station tags on the circuit. img: public-domain picture id; x:true = the exact work discussed, otherwise an illustration. */
const STATIONS=[
{id:'studio',n:'The studio',k:'First price',brief:'The first price is set in a conversation between an artist and a dealer, and it anchors every number that follows. The gallery–artist split has been 50/50 for a century; the artist usually sees nothing from resales in the United States, and a capped royalty in Europe.',money:'Dealer pays the artist half of the net price, often months later.',check:'Who set the primary price, and when?',img:'pic-yo',cap:'Yo, Picasso (1901) hung in Picasso’s first Paris show, at Ambroise Vollard’s gallery, the year the artist turned twenty.'},
{id:'gallery',n:'Primary gallery',k:'Discounts, lists, invoices',brief:'Galleries ration access rather than raise prices: waiting lists, museum-first placement, 10% collector discounts and 15–20% for institutions. The invoice may carry a three-to-five-year resale ban, a right of first refusal, and a “buy one, give one” to a museum.',money:'Collector pays list less discount; the dealer absorbs the discount from its half.',check:'Read the invoice terms before the wire.',img:'pic-basket',x:true,cap:'Fillette à la corbeille fleurie: Leo and Gertrude Stein paid 150 francs in 1905; the Rockefeller estate sold it for $115m in 2018.'},
{id:'collector',n:'The collector’s wall',k:'Use, tax, insurance',brief:'Where the picture hangs decides the tax: New York City adds 8.875% if it is delivered there; a “resale certificate” is a crime if it goes home. Insurance runs 0.1–0.5% of agreed value a year. The flip is the quiet way out: a private resale to another collector.',money:'Sales or use tax, insurance, and a possible private resale.',check:'Does the shipping paper match the real delivery address?',img:'klimt-adele2',x:true,cap:'Adele Bloch-Bauer II: restituted to Maria Altmann and sold at Christie’s in 2006 for $87.9m to Oprah Winfrey (reported); resold privately in 2016 for a reported ≈$150m.'},
{id:'advisor',n:'Advisor or dealer',k:'Principal or agent?',brief:'The largest losses in the modern market came from an intermediary who was treated as an agent but acted as a principal. Yves Bouvier bought 38 works for about $2bn for one client with markups above $1bn; Lisa Schiff took clients’ money against works she never paid for.',money:'A fee of 5–10% as agent, or an undisclosed markup as principal.',check:'A written statement of role and cost, before any payment.',img:'gauguin-tefare',x:true,cap:'Te Fare: Rybolovlev’s trusts paid a reported $85m in 2008, through Bouvier by their account; Christie’s London sold it on 28 Feb 2017 for £20.3m ($25.2m). The difference priced the affair.'},
{id:'freeport',n:'The freeport',k:'Bonded, suspended, stored',brief:'A customs warehouse where VAT and duty are suspended for as long as the goods stay. Geneva holds more than 1.2 million works; since 2016 its operators must keep an inventory with owners’ names. Luxembourg lost clients when it started asking who owned the goods.',money:'Storage fees instead of tax; nothing is due until the work leaves.',check:'Obtain the inventory record and the declared owner.',img:'klimt-serpents',x:true,cap:'Water Serpents II: Bouvier paid $112m in 2012 and sold it to Rybolovlev for $183.8m in 2013; it was resold in 2015 for about $170m, having lived in storage rather than on a wall.'},
{id:'company',n:'Companies and trusts',k:'Who owns it?',brief:'Trophies are owned by companies in Panama, the BVI or Delaware, or by family trusts. The structure is legal; the opacity is the risk, because a sanctions check, a restitution claim or a divorce looks through it. The Panama Papers exposed a Modigliani this way in 2016.',money:'The company sells, the beneficiary is screened, the price is private.',check:'Identify the ultimate beneficial owner with 50% look-through.',img:'vg-gachet',x:true,cap:'Portrait of Dr Gachet: $82.5m in 1990 to Ryoei Saito, resold privately in the late 1990s, and not seen in public since. The owner is unconfirmed.'},
{id:'lender',n:'The lender',k:'50% of the appraisal',brief:'Loans secured on art stood at $34–40bn going into 2026. Lenders advance about half of a conservative appraisal, file a UCC-1 lien, and must consent to any sale. A guarantee at auction is also a financing: the guarantor is paid a fee if outbid.',money:'Interest from 2–3% at private banks to 9%+ at specialists.',check:'Run a lien search before you buy; budget lender consent before you sell.',img:'modi-nu-gauche',x:true,cap:'Nu couché (sur le côté gauche): sold at Sotheby’s in 2018 for $157.2m on a single bid to the party that had guaranteed it.'},
{id:'museum',n:'The museum loan',k:'Revocable, seizable',brief:'A long-term loan flatters a work’s provenance and can lower a collector’s tax, and it can be recalled: the Staechelin Gauguin left the Kunstmuseum Basel after a ≈$210m sale. A loan can also be seized: Schiele’s Portrait of Wally was taken in New York in 1998 and settled for $19m in 2010.',money:'Insurance and conservation paid by the museum; no price, usually.',check:'Immunity-from-seizure status, and the loan agreement’s recall terms.',img:'schiele-wally',x:true,cap:'Portrait of Wally Neuzil: seized on loan from the Leopold collection to MoMA in 1998; the Leopold Museum paid the Bondi heirs $19m in 2010 to keep it.'},
{id:'fair',n:'The fair',k:'Carnets and booths',brief:'Fairs move a third of dealer sales. Works travel on ATA carnets, which allow duty-free temporary import for a year but forbid sale: selling off a carnet triggers 110% of the duties. The booth is where the primary and the secondary market meet in public.',money:'Booth fees, shipping and insurance; sales invoiced from home jurisdictions.',check:'Was the work entered on a carnet? A sale needs a customs entry.',img:'canaletto-marco',cap:'Canaletto, Piazza San Marco. Venice ran a sales office at its Biennale until 1968; the modern fair began where the modern tourist did.'},
{id:'border',n:'The border',k:'Licences, VAT, duty',brief:'Export licences can stop a sale: the UK defers, France declares a trésor national, Italy notifies. Import VAT is 5% in the UK, 5.5% in France, 0% in Hong Kong; the United States charges no duty on art and exempts it from the 2026 Section 301 duties, except Chinese-origin works (7.5%) and Canadian-origin works (listed for 50%).',money:'Import VAT on the price; the licence can cost the deal.',check:'Classify the object (HTS 9701–9705 or 9706) and confirm the licence before quoting.',img:'rem-soolmans',x:true,cap:'Rembrandt’s pendants of Marten Soolmans and Oopjen Coppit: the Rothschild family’s sale became a €160m joint purchase by France and the Netherlands in 2016, one portrait each.'},
{id:'auction',n:'The auction room',k:'Reserve, guarantee, symbols',brief:'The reserve cannot exceed the low estimate; the auctioneer may bid for the seller up to it. In 2025, 78.3% of New York evening-sale value was guaranteed. The catalogue symbols say who already has a stake: ○ guaranteed, ⋑ irrevocable bid, ¤ or Δ an interested party.',money:'Hammer to the seller less commission; premium to the house; a fee to the guarantor.',check:'Read that sale’s symbol key and ask the guaranteed share.',img:'modi-nu',x:true,cap:'Nu couché: $170.4m at Christie’s in 2015 to Liu Yiqian, who reportedly paid with an American Express card and hung it in his museum in Shanghai.'},
{id:'private',n:'Private treaty',k:'No room, no record',brief:'Above $100m, most sales are private: Cézanne’s Card Players to Qatar at ≈$250m, Gauguin’s Nafea at ≈$210m, Griffin’s $500m from Geffen. The houses’ private-sales desks made $1.8bn in the first half of 2026. No public price, no public buyer, and the same symbols do not apply.',money:'Negotiated commission on one side or both; prices surface in court filings, if ever.',check:'Who is the seller of record, and who else has an economic interest?',img:'gauguin-nafea',x:true,cap:'Nafea faa ipoipo: Rudolf Staechelin’s family trust sold it in 2015 for a reported ≈$210m to Qatar, and withdrew it from the Kunstmuseum Basel, where it had hung for decades.'},
{id:'estate',n:'The estate',k:'40% above $15m',brief:'Death is the market’s largest consignor. A US estate pays 40% above the 2026 exemption of $15m a person; a gift to a museum is deductible; a private foundation keeps the art and the deduction together. The IRS Art Advisory Panel reads any appraisal above $50,000.',money:'Estate tax, or a deduction, or a sale to pay the tax.',check:'Qualified appraisal, defensible comparables, and the foundation’s public-benefit record.',img:'bott-roundel',x:true,cap:'Young Man Holding a Roundel: bought by Sheldon Solow in 1982 for £810,000 and sold by his estate in 2021 for $92.2m.'},
{id:'restitution',n:'Restitution and seizure',k:'The 1933–45 gap',brief:'A provenance gap between 1933 and 1945 in Europe, or an antiquity without pre-1970 export papers, can void a sale decades later. The Nahmad Modigliani was ordered returned in April 2026 after a 1996 purchase; the Manhattan DA has recovered more than 6,100 antiquities worth $480m.',money:'The work goes back; the money rarely does.',check:'Run Lost Art and lootedart.com; check the Manhattan DA and museum return lists.',img:'kand-bunte',x:true,cap:'Das bunte Leben: deposited at the Stedelijk in 1933, auctioned in 1940, restituted to the Lewenstein heirs in 2023, and offered at Christie’s London on 14 Oct 2026 for more than $30m.'},
{id:'fakes',n:'Attribution and fakes',k:'Who says so?',brief:'Knoedler sold more than $80m of fakes painted in Queens; Beltracchi invented a collection. The authentication boards for Warhol, Basquiat and Haring have closed under the cost of litigation. An auction guarantee of authenticity lasts five years and belongs to the original buyer only.',money:'The whole price, when the attribution falls.',check:'Catalogue raisonné entry, technical analysis, and a written warranty.',img:'leo-salvator',x:true,cap:'Salvator Mundi: £45 in 1958 as a follower’s work, $450.3m in 2017 as a Leonardo; the Prado’s 2021 catalogue listed it under the workshop.'},
{id:'screening',n:'Sanctions and AML',k:'Who is paying?',brief:'In the EU and UK any €10,000 art transaction triggers customer due diligence; the United States has no art-market rule, so banks and houses run their own. The Rotenbergs bought $18m of art through shells after the 2014 sanctions; a London dealer went to prison in 2025 for selling £140,000 of art to a Hezbollah financier.',money:'Frozen funds, voided sales, and fines of £1,350 to £158,679 so far in the UK.',check:'Screen the beneficial owner on OFAC, UK and EU lists above $100,000.'}
];

/* the atlas: rules and cases. kind: 'rule' or 'case'. year for cases. */
const ATLAS=[
// freeports
{id:'geneva',kind:'rule',st:['freeport'],t:'Geneva: 1.2 million works, one inventory since 2016',b:'The Swiss Federal Audit Office counted more than 1.2 million works of art in the Geneva freeport. The often-quoted $100bn of stored value is a media estimate repeated in the European Parliament’s 2018 study, not an audited figure. Since 1 January 2016 operators must keep an inventory recording goods and their owners, exporters must declare goods bound for a freeport and the buyer’s identity, and goods stored for export are limited to six months unless customs extends the term.',c:'Treat any “value stored” figure as an estimate. Ask the counterparty for the freeport inventory entry and the declared owner.',v:'V',img:'klimt-serpents',src:[['European Parliament, EPRS 2018 (PDF)','https://www.europarl.europa.eu/cmsdata/155721/EPRS_STUD_627114_Money%20laundering-FINAL.pdf'],['Artnet: Switzerland’s freeport rules','https://news.artnet.com/market/switzerland-freeport-regulations-367361']]},
{id:'luxembourg',kind:'rule',st:['freeport','screening'],t:'Luxembourg lost clients when it asked who owned the goods',b:'Le Freeport at Findel airport opened in September 2014, majority-owned by Yves Bouvier. It was the first freeport subject to anti-money-laundering duties, from July 2015, with full identification of beneficial owners from 1 August 2016. The European Parliament’s study records that it then lost “twenty to thirty long-standing clients”, who moved to “other free ports with more discretion”. It rebranded as the Luxembourg High Security Hub on 1 January 2021 and now stores precious metals, pharmaceuticals and strategic equipment.',c:'A Luxembourg storage record carries a beneficial-owner check. A Geneva record from before 2016 may not exist.',v:'V',src:[['EPRS 2018, pp. 27–31','https://www.europarl.europa.eu/cmsdata/155721/EPRS_STUD_627114_Money%20laundering-FINAL.pdf'],['Fortius: the High Security Hub','https://fortius.lu/en/blog/le-freeport-becomes-the-luxembourg-high-security-hub/']]},
{id:'singapore',kind:'rule',st:['freeport','tax'],t:'Singapore sold cheap; Delaware sells a postcode',b:'Bouvier sold Singapore’s Le Freeport at Changi, built around 2010 for about $70m, to the crypto investor Jihan Wu for a reported $28.4m in September 2022. The Delaware Freeport, founded in 2015 by the shipper Fritz Dietl, markets the avoidance of New York and Florida sales tax by direct shipment to a state with none; its founder has conceded that its foreign-trade-zone status “saves little money”, since art carries no federal duty. Beijing’s “Freeport of Culture” was announced around 2012 as an $800m hub; its operations are unconfirmed.',c:'In the United States the delivery state decides sales and use tax, not the word freeport.',v:'V',src:[['The Art Newspaper, 20 Sep 2022','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2022/09/20/swiss-art-dealer-yves-bouvier-sells-singapore-freeport-to-chinese-crypto-billionaire-jihan-wu-for-reported-284m'],['Longreads: Tax-free storage wars','https://longreads.com/2018/05/29/tax-free-storage-wars/']]},
{id:'euzones',kind:'rule',st:['freeport','screening'],t:'82 free zones, and the warehouses outside them',b:'The European Parliament’s 2018 study counted 82 free zones in the EU and warned that ordinary customs warehouses, a far larger market, sat outside the anti-money-laundering directive. The Council adopted the 2024 package on 30 May 2024: a single rulebook applying from mid-2027, the sixth directive, a new authority (AMLA) in Frankfurt from mid-2025, and an EU-wide €10,000 cap on cash payments. Art dealers and freeport operators were already obliged entities under the fifth directive.',c:'In the EU, any art transaction of €10,000 or more, including storage or intermediation in a free zone, triggers customer due diligence.',v:'V',src:[['EPRS study page','https://www.europarl.europa.eu/thinktank/en/document/EPRS_STU(2018)627114'],['Council of the EU, 30 May 2024','https://www.consilium.europa.eu/en/press/press-releases/2024/05/30/anti-money-laundering-council-adopts-package-of-rules/']]},
// AML & sanctions
{id:'amld5',kind:'rule',st:['screening','gallery','auction'],t:'€10,000: the line that makes a dealer a bank',b:'Directive 2018/843 added to the EU’s money-laundering rules “persons trading or acting as intermediaries in the trade of works of art, including when this is carried out by art galleries and auction houses, where the value of the transaction or a series of linked transactions amounts to EUR 10 000 or more”, and the same for persons storing or trading art in free ports. Member states had to transpose it by 10 January 2020.',c:'Linked transactions aggregate. Splitting an invoice does not escape the threshold.',v:'V',src:[['EUR-Lex: Directive 2018/843','https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:32018L0843']]},
{id:'hmrc',kind:'rule',st:['screening','gallery'],t:'HMRC is fining the late registrants',b:'UK “art market participants” must register with HMRC for transactions of €10,000 or more. HMRC data for 1 April to 30 September 2025 show 35 penalties against art market participants, 34 of them for late registration, the largest £36,400 and the smallest £1,350. The largest published fine is £158,679 against DYS44 Art Gallery Ltd in September 2025, for failures in risk assessment, policies, due diligence and record-keeping. The number of registered participants is not published.',c:'Confirm your counterparty’s HMRC registration number before any UK deal above €10,000.',v:'V',src:[['Stephenson Harwood: HMRC AML data','https://www.stephensonharwood.com/insights/aml-and-the-art-market-hmrc-data-on-enforcement-and-key-takeaways/'],['The Art Newspaper, 23 Sep 2025','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2025/09/23/money-laundering-crackdown-continues-as-dealer-faces-a-fine-of-more-than-%C2%A3150000']]},
{id:'usaml',kind:'rule',st:['screening','auction','private'],t:'The United States still has no art-market rule',b:'The Anti-Money Laundering Act of 2020 extended the Bank Secrecy Act to dealers in antiquities and ordered a rule. FinCEN published an advance notice on 24 September 2021; the proposed rule was never published and the notice was withdrawn, as Senator Fetterman’s letter of 3 June 2026 to Treasury records. Treasury’s February 2022 study of the art market found limited evidence and offered options rather than a rule; the broader Art Market Integrity Act has been introduced but not passed. As of October 2026 no US art- or antiquities-market AML rule is in force.',c:'In the US the checks are the banks’ and the houses’ own. Expect source-of-funds questions anyway, and ask them yourself.',v:'V',src:[['Sen. Fetterman letter, June 2026 (PDF)','https://www.fetterman.senate.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026.05.31-Antiquities-Rule-Letter-DRAFT.docx-1.pdf'],['Federal Register, 24 Sep 2021','https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2021/09/24/2021-20731/anti-money-laundering-regulations-for-dealers-in-antiquities'],['Harvard HALO: US and Swiss law compared','https://orgs.law.harvard.edu/halo/2025/03/13/fighting-money-laundering-in-the-art-market-a-comparative-overview-of-u-s-and-swiss-law/']]},
{id:'swiss',kind:'rule',st:['screening','freeport'],t:'Switzerland: CHF 100,000 in cash',b:'Swiss art dealers fall under the Anti-Money Laundering Act only when they accept cash payments above CHF 100,000 in a transaction (Article 8a). Parliament revised the act on 26 September 2025: dealers in precious metals and stones drop to a CHF 15,000 cash threshold, while the CHF 100,000 threshold for other traders, art dealers included, stays. Hong Kong names only dealers in precious metals and stones; mainland China’s anti-money-laundering law does not name art dealers.',c:'A Swiss cash deal under CHF 100,000 carries no statutory due diligence. Impose your own.',v:'V',src:[['Harvard HALO: US and Swiss law compared','https://orgs.law.harvard.edu/halo/2025/03/13/fighting-money-laundering-in-the-art-market-a-comparative-overview-of-u-s-and-swiss-law/'],['Lenz & Staehelin: AMLA amendments adopted 26 Sep 2025','https://www.lenzstaehelin.com/fr/news-insights/browse-thought-leadership-insights/insights-detail/amendments-to-the-swiss-anti-money-laundering-act/'],['Chambers 2026: art and cultural property law, China','https://practiceguides.chambers.com/practice-guides/art-cultural-property-law-2026/china']]},
{id:'rotenberg',kind:'case',year:2020,st:['screening','company','auction'],t:'The Rotenbergs bought $18m of art through shells',b:'The Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations reported on 29 July 2020 that after the March 2014 sanctions Arkady and Boris Rotenberg bought more than $18m of art in transactions touching the US financial system, through the Moscow agent Gregory Baltser and shell companies in the BVI and Belize, with linked shells in nearly $91m of transactions. The houses examined were Sotheby’s, Christie’s, Phillips and Bonhams. The report called the art trade “the largest, legal unregulated industry in the United States”. OFAC’s advisory of 30 October 2020 said the “informational materials” exemption does not cover art dealings with blocked persons and urged particular caution above $100,000.',c:'Screen the ultimate beneficial owner, not the agent or the shell, with 50% look-through.',v:'V',src:[['Cohen & Gresser on the Senate report (PDF)','https://www.cohengresser.com/app/uploads/2020/09/Senate-Report-Exposes-the-U.S.-Art-Market-as-a-Hotbed-for-Money-Laundering-and-U.S.-Sanctions-Evasion.pdf'],['Debevoise summary (PDF)','https://www.debevoise.com/-/media/files/insights/publications/2020/08/20200827-senate-subcommittee-report-highlights.pdf'],['Ropes & Gray on the OFAC advisory','https://www.ropesgray.com/en/insights/alerts/2020/11/ofac-issues-art-advisory-highlighting-sanctions-risk-in-the-art-trade']]},
{id:'luxuryban',kind:'rule',st:['screening','border'],t:'£250: the UK luxury-goods ban',b:'The Russia (Sanctions) (EU Exit) (Amendment) (No. 8) Regulations 2022, in force 14 April 2022, prohibit the export or supply of luxury goods, including works of art and antiques worth £250 or more excluding VAT, to Russia or to persons connected with Russia: resident there 180 days a year or more, located or incorporated there. Petr Aven and Alisher Usmanov were EU-listed on 28 February 2022; Moshe Kantor was delisted in March 2025. Phillips, owned since 2008 by Mercury Group’s Leonid Friedland and Leonid Strunin, who are not sanctioned, gave its £5.8m share of its 3 March 2022 London sale to the Ukrainian Red Cross.',c:'Almost any artwork to a Russia-resident buyer is prohibited from the UK regardless of list status. Re-screen at every stage; lists move.',v:'V',src:[['legislation.gov.uk: SI 2022/452','https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2022/452/made'],['ArtAML: the luxury goods ban','https://knowledgebase.artaml.com/what-is-the-luxury-goods-ban'],['The Art Newspaper, 3 Mar 2022','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2022/03/03/phillips-donates-share-from-london-sale-ukraine-red-cross']]},
{id:'ahmad',kind:'case',year:2025,st:['screening','gallery'],t:'A dealer jailed for selling to Hezbollah’s financier',b:'On 18 April 2023 prosecutors in New York unsealed a nine-count indictment against Nazem Ahmad, a designated Hezbollah financier, and eight associates, citing more than $400m of transactions between January 2020 and August 2022, about $160m of them through US finance, and more than $450,000 of art bought from US sources after his designation. In London the television art expert Oghenochuko Ojiri was sentenced at the Old Bailey on 6 June 2025 to two and a half years for eight Terrorism Act offences: about £140,000 of art sold to Ahmad between October 2020 and December 2021, the first prosecution of its kind.',c:'Failing to report a suspicious buyer is itself the offence. Screen the buyer, not only the seller.',v:'V',src:[['US Department of Justice, 18 Apr 2023','https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/ofac-designated-hezbollah-financier-and-eight-associates-charged-multiple-crimes-arising-out'],['Courthouse News on the Ojiri sentence','https://www.courthousenews.com/a-british-tv-art-expert-who-sold-works-to-a-suspected-hezbollah-financier-is-sentenced-to-prison/']]},
{id:'1mdb',kind:'case',year:2017,st:['screening','company','collector'],t:'1MDB: the forfeiture followed the pictures',b:'On 16 June 2017 Leonardo DiCaprio surrendered a Picasso still life ($3.2m) and Basquiat’s Redman One ($9m), gifts from the 1MDB circle, the day the Department of Justice filed a complaint seeking $540m and citing “upwards of $100m” in art. A settlement of about $700m in assets in October 2019 included Monet’s Saint-Georges majeur (over $20m) and Van Gogh’s La Maison de Vincent à Arles ($5.5m). In July 2024 the department recovered nearly $85m more, including works by Picasso, Van Gogh, Monet, Basquiat and Arbus; more than $1.4bn has been returned to Malaysia out of an alleged $4.5bn misappropriated.',c:'Screen the source of funds and any gift chain. Forfeiture follows art into third-party hands.',v:'V',src:[['Artnet, 2017','https://news.artnet.com/art-world/leonardo-dicaprio-gives-pack-jho-low-picasso-basquiat-996377'],['US Department of Justice, Jul 2024','https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/justice-department-recover-nearly-85m-additional-funds-linked-1mdb-scheme']]},
// tax
{id:'nyag',kind:'case',year:2024,st:['tax','auction','collector'],t:'Sotheby’s and the resale certificates',b:'On 14 November 2024 Sotheby’s paid $6.25m to settle the New York Attorney General’s suit over false resale certificates accepted from 2010 to 2020, notably from Porsal Equities (“the Collector”, Isaac Sultan), which bought about $27m of art between 2010 and 2015 and had settled separately for more than $10m in 2018. At least seven other clients were involved; staff sometimes filled in the forms. Sotheby’s must now train staff and verify resale intent. New York City’s combined rate is 8.875%.',c:'A resale certificate is valid only if the buyer genuinely holds the work for resale. Never for a home.',v:'V',src:[['NY Attorney General, 14 Nov 2024','https://ag.ny.gov/press-release/2024/attorney-general-james-and-acting-dtf-commissioner-hiller-secure-more-6-million']]},
{id:'kozlowski',kind:'case',year:2002,st:['tax','collector','logistics'],t:'Empty crates to New Hampshire',b:'On 4 June 2002 the Manhattan District Attorney indicted Tyco’s chief executive Dennis Kozlowski for evading about $1m of New York sales tax on about $13m of paintings, including Monet’s Monte Carlo, vue de Cap Martin ($3.9m), by shipping empty crates to Tyco’s New Hampshire office while the pictures went to his Fifth Avenue apartment; a consultant’s memo read “(wink, wink)”. The tax charges were resolved on 12 May 2006 by a $21.2m settlement covering $3.2m of sales tax on $15m of art and $18m of income tax. He was separately convicted of grand larceny in 2005.',c:'Shipping papers must match the physical delivery address.',v:'V',src:[['Forbes, 4 Jun 2002','https://www.forbes.com/2002/06/04/0604kozlowski.html'],['NBC News, 2006','https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna12759538']]},
{id:'1031',kind:'rule',st:['tax','collector'],t:'Like-kind is dead',b:'The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 limited Section 1031 exchanges to real property from 1 January 2018, ending the tax-deferred art-for-art swap that collectors had used to roll gains into new purchases.',c:'No deferral on art-for-art trades since 2018; a sale is a taxable event.',v:'S',src:[['Tax Notes: like-kind exchanges limited to real property','https://www.taxnotes.com/research/federal/other-documents/irs-news-releases/like-kind-exchanges-now-limited-to-real-property/28m7h']]},
{id:'irspanel',kind:'rule',st:['tax','estate'],t:'The Art Advisory Panel reads your appraisal',b:'Items claimed at $50,000 or more on US estate, gift or charitable returns are referred to the IRS Art Advisory Panel. In fiscal 2023 its 17 members reviewed 195 items in 37 cases with a claimed value of $795.5m, an average of $4.08m an item; they accepted 53% of the values and recommended a net reduction of $16.9m, about 2%. The IRS unit adopted only 63% of the recommendations in full. Later years are not yet published.',c:'A qualified appraisal above $50,000 will be read by the Panel. Make the comparables defensible.',v:'V',src:[['IRS Publication 5392 (PDF)','https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/p5392.pdf']]},
{id:'estatetax',kind:'rule',st:['tax','estate'],t:'40% above $15m',b:'The 2025 federal estate-tax exemption was $13.99m a person. The One Big Beautiful Bill Act, signed 4 July 2025, sets it at $15m a person from 1 January 2026, indexed and without a sunset; the top rate of 40% applies above the exemption. Art in an estate is taxed on appraised fair market value.',c:'Art in a US estate above $15m ($30m for a married couple) is taxed at 40% on appraised value. Plan the consignment, the gift or the foundation before the appraisal.',v:'V',src:[['Morgan Lewis, Aug 2025','https://www.morganlewis.com/pubs/2025/08/estate-tax-alert-new-15-million-federal-exemption-becomes-law']]},
{id:'ukarr',kind:'rule',st:['tax','border','auction'],t:'ARR and the 5% import VAT',b:'From 1 April 2024 the UK artist’s resale right applies to sales of £1,000 or more: 4% up to £50,000, 3% to £200,000, 1% to £350,000, 0.5% to £500,000 and 0.25% above, capped at £12,500 a sale, for the artist’s life plus 70 years. It is paid only for nationals of the UK, the EEA and the countries listed in Schedule 2 of the 2006 Regulations: American, Chinese, Japanese and Swiss artists receive none. The seller and the seller’s agent are liable in law; the London houses charge it to the buyer in their conditions of sale. UK import VAT on art is effectively 5%: the value is taken at 25% and taxed at 20% (VAT Act 1994, s. 21(4)).',c:'Add ARR (up to £12,500) for a qualifying artist and 5% import VAT to any UK secondary-market landed cost.',v:'V',src:[['gov.uk: Artist’s Resale Right','https://www.gov.uk/guidance/artists-resale-right'],['The Artist’s Resale Right Regulations 2006 (SI 2006/346), reg. 10 and Schedule 2','https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2006/346'],['HMRC manual IMPS05200','https://gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/imports/imps05200']]},
{id:'frarr',kind:'rule',st:['tax','auction'],t:'The droit de suite is the buyer’s now',b:'France’s resale right (droit de suite, Code de la propriété intellectuelle art. L122-8) runs from €750 on the EU scale, 4% falling to 0.25%, capped at €12,500 a sale, for the artist’s life plus 70 years. The law puts it on the seller. Christie’s France moved it to the buyer in its conditions of sale; the EU Court of Justice allowed such a clause on 26 February 2015 (C-41/14) and the Cour de cassation upheld it in November 2018. Phillips’ Paris guide charges it to the buyer on the hammer. An artist from outside the EU and EEA qualifies only if the artist’s country grants the right in return, or by ministerial order after five years’ residence and a part in French artistic life: the first orders, for Takesada Matsutani and the heirs of Sam Francis, came on 30 October 2024.',c:'In Paris, add the royalty (up to €12,500) to the buyer’s cost of a work by a qualifying artist within the term; for an American, Chinese, Japanese or Swiss artist it is not due.',v:'S',src:[['Phillips: Paris buyer’s guide','https://dist.phillips.com/content/web/docs/forms/Buyers_Guide_Paris_FR_ENG.pdf'],['Kluwer Copyright Blog: auction houses may transfer the royalty to the buyer (C-41/14), 27 Feb 2015','https://legalblogs.wolterskluwer.com/copyright-blog/ecj-auction-houses-may-transfer-cost-of-artists-resale-royalties-to-the-buyer/'],['Art Critique: the French supreme court rules for Christie’s France, 5 Jan 2019','https://www.art-critique.com/en/2019/01/french-supreme-court-rules-in-favour-of-christies-france/'],['Squire Patton Boggs, La Revue: the first foreign artists admitted, 10 Feb 2025','https://larevue.squirepattonboggs.com/le-ministere-de-la-culture-admet-pour-la-premiere-fois-des-artistes-etrangers-au-benefice-du-droit-de-suite-en-france.html']]},
{id:'euvat',kind:'rule',st:['tax','border','gallery'],t:'VAT rates move the price by up to 14 points',b:'Germany cut VAT on gallery and dealer sales of art from 19% to 7% on 1 January 2025, restoring a benefit removed in 2014. France applies 5.5% to imports and, since 1 January 2025, to works of art sold outside the margin scheme by any seller; margin-scheme sales pay 20% on the margin. Italy cut its rate to 5% from 1 July 2025, including imports; the Netherlands charges 9% and Belgium 6%. The EU resale right is a 4% sliding scale capped at €12,500.',c:'The VAT rate follows where the work is imported or sold. Route choice is a price term.',v:'V',src:[['Artsy: Germany cuts VAT to 7%','https://www.artsy.net/article/artsy-editorial-germany-cuts-vat-artwork-sales-7'],['Barnebys: VAT in 2025','https://www.barnebys.co.uk/blog/what-art-buyers-need-to-know-about-vat-in-2025'],['BOFiP: BOI-TVA-SECT-90-40, 5.5% on works of art from 1 Jan 2025','https://bofip.impots.gouv.fr/bofip/1285-PGP.html/identifiant=BOI-TVA-SECT-90-40-20250514']]},
{id:'exportvat',kind:'rule',st:['tax','border','freeport'],t:'Exported, the VAT comes back',b:'At Phillips a lot that leaves the sale room’s country is sold free of that country’s VAT once the export is proved, on terms that differ by room. In London the VAT on the premium is cancelled or refunded against export papers stamped by HMRC within three months of the sale; for an imported lot, the 5% import VAT on the hammer and the 20% on the premium, if the lot leaves within thirty days of payment. In Paris a lot exported outside the EU within three months by a carrier Phillips has approved, instructed by the time of invoicing, is invoiced free of VAT; otherwise the buyer pays a deposit equal to the VAT, refunded on proof endorsed by French customs within three months, and a lot collected in person pays the French VAT in full, even if it is exported later. In Geneva the 8.1% on hammer and premium is refunded to a buyer domiciled outside Switzerland on an export declaration stamped by Swiss customs. Swiss customs count a delivery straight into a free warehouse, such as the Geneva freeport, as a direct export, the condition for selling free of VAT.',c:'For a buyer who exports, the VAT is a deposit, not a cost, on the house’s own terms. Late or faulty papers, a hand-carried lot or the wrong domicile make it a cost. Ask each house for its terms before the sale.',v:'S',src:[['Phillips: UK tax guide, export refunds (updated 17 Oct 2022)','https://phillips.com/buysell/london/vat'],['Phillips: French VAT guide, export invoices and deposits, 1 Oct 2026','https://dist.phillips.com/content/web/docs/forms/VAT_Guide_FR_ENG.pdf'],['Phillips: Geneva guide for prospective buyers, VAT and refunds, effective 12 Apr 2026','https://dist.phillips.com/content/web/docs/forms/Guide_for_Prospective_Buyers_GVA.pdf'],['BAZG: VAT-exempt supply on export (a free warehouse counts as a direct export)','https://www.bazg.admin.ch/de/mehrwertsteuer-steuerbefreite-lieferung-bei-ausfuhr'],['FOCBS: publication 52.22, import tax on works of art, Jan 2022','https://www.bazg.admin.ch/dam/de/sd-web/DVzCHes9O-wq/52_22_einfuhrsteueraufkunstwerken.pdf']]},
{id:'tariffs',kind:'rule',st:['border','tax'],t:'Duty-free into the United States, with two exceptions',b:'Paintings, prints, sculpture, collectors’ pieces and antiques (HTSUS chapter 97) enter the United States free of duty. In 2025 they were also exempt from the IEEPA tariffs as “informational materials” under 50 U.S.C. §1702(b), which Customs and Border Protection confirmed on 27 May 2025; the Section 301 duties announced in July 2026 exempt artworks too (heading 9903.05.92). Two exceptions remain: Chinese-origin art still carries the 7.5% Section 301 duty of 2020, and Canadian-origin chapter 97 goods were listed for 50% under Section 338 from August 2026. Design and furniture (chapter 94) are not art for these purposes. Hong Kong levies no import duty or VAT on art; mainland China applies 13% VAT, and its specific customs duty on paintings is unconfirmed.',c:'Classify the object (9701–9705, 9706 or 9403) and name its country of origin before quoting a landed price; check the current Section 301 and 338 annexes, which change by notice. Hong Kong delivery is the zero-tax route into Greater China; mainland delivery is not.',v:'V',src:[['USITC: HTSUS 2026, chapter 97 (every line free)','https://learning.usitc.gov/hts-docs/documents/Chapter%2097.pdf'],['CBP CSMS #69326983: the July 2026 Section 301 duties exempt artworks (9903.05.92)','https://content.govdelivery.com/accounts/USDHSCBP/bulletins/421d887'],['White House: Section 338 annex, Canadian chapter 97 lines at +50% (9903.03.14)','https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Annex-II-2.pdf'],['Fieldfisher: tariffs and the art world (the 2025 exemption)','https://www.fieldfisher.com/en/insights/tariffs-and-the-art-world-what-is-going-on'],['Artnet on the China tariffs','https://news.artnet.com/market/trumps-tariff-war-china-hong-kong-art-2608618']]},
{id:'carnet',kind:'rule',st:['fair','border','logistics'],t:'A carnet cannot sell',b:'An ATA Carnet allows duty-free temporary import of exhibition and fair goods for one year across more than 100 territories, but goods “intended for sale or sale on approval cannot be entered on a carnet”; selling triggers 110% of the duties. Climate-controlled storage runs from about $0.50 to more than $5 per cubic foot a month, with climate control roughly doubling the basic fee.',c:'A work at a fair on a carnet cannot legally be sold without a customs entry. Ask how it travelled.',v:'V',src:[['CBP: ATA Carnet FAQs','https://cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/entry-summary/ata-carnet-faqs'],['Renwick: storage costs','https://renwickfas.com/knowledge/75914/']]},
// auction mechanics
{id:'symbols',kind:'rule',st:['auction','lender'],t:'Read the symbols',b:'Sotheby’s New York: “○ Guaranteed Property: the seller of lots with this symbol has been guaranteed a minimum price”; “⋑ Irrevocable Bids: a party has provided Sotheby’s with an irrevocable bid … that ensures that the lot will sell”. The irrevocable bidder may bid higher and “may be compensated … by receiving a contingent fee, a fixed fee or both”. Christie’s uses ° for a house guarantee, °♦ for a third-party guarantee and ¤ for an interested party; Phillips ° house, ♦ third party, ⋑ irrevocable bid, Δ ownership interest (Phillips’ Δ and Σ confirmed in its own conditions; the Christie’s wording is from secondary sources).',c:'Read the symbol key of that sale’s catalogue, not a generic guide. Symbols change by house and season.',v:'V',src:[['Sotheby’s New York conditions (PDF)','https://www.sothebys.com/en/en/docs/pdf/11-27-23-ny-en'],['Phillips conditions of sale (PDF)','https://www.dist.phillips.com/content/web/docs/forms/UK_Online_Condition_of_Sale.pdf'],['Artsy: catalogue symbols decoded','https://www.artsy.net/article/artsy-editorial-auction-catalogue-symbols-decoded']]},
{id:'reserve',kind:'rule',st:['auction'],t:'The reserve and the auctioneer’s own bids',b:'Sotheby’s: “The reserve … will not exceed the low estimate”; “The auctioneer may open the bidding on any lot by placing a bid on behalf of the seller” and “may further bid on behalf of the seller, up to the amount of the reserve, by placing responsive or consecutive bids”. London: “The Reserve for a Lot cannot exceed the Lot’s low estimate.” Phillips: the reserve will not exceed the low estimate unless a third-party irrevocable bid is higher, in which case “the lot’s reserve may be set at the amount of the irrevocable bid”.',c:'Below the low estimate, assume the bids may be the auctioneer’s. Ask whether an irrevocable bid has lifted the reserve.',v:'V',src:[['Sotheby’s New York conditions (PDF)','https://www.sothebys.com/en/en/docs/pdf/11-27-23-ny-en'],['Sotheby’s London conditions (PDF)','https://www.sothebys.com/conditions-of-business-for-buyers-london-02.07.2024.pdf']]},
{id:'warranty',kind:'rule',st:['auction','fakes'],t:'Five years, original buyer only',b:'Sotheby’s London guarantees against “counterfeit” for “five years after the date of the auction”, for the buyer only and non-transferable; the sole remedy is rescission and refund of the purchase price, excluded where the description matched accepted scholarship at the time or detection would have been unreasonably expensive. Late payment carries interest at 6% above the HSBC base rate; Phillips charges 12% a year and gives a five-year authorship warranty. Christie’s five-year warranty is standard but its current wording was not fetched.',c:'The guarantee belongs to the original buyer. It does not travel with a private resale.',v:'V',src:[['Sotheby’s London conditions (PDF)','https://www.sothebys.com/conditions-of-business-for-buyers-london-02.07.2024.pdf'],['Phillips conditions of sale (PDF)','https://www.dist.phillips.com/content/web/docs/forms/UK_Online_Condition_of_Sale.pdf']]},
{id:'jargon',kind:'rule',st:['auction'],t:'Estimate on request, bought in, white glove, 105',b:'“Estimate on request” is used for very high lots, with press guidance “in the region of”; a “bought in” lot failed to reach its reserve and is offered after the sale by staff; chandelier bidding is the auctioneer’s fictitious bids up to the reserve; “white glove” means 100% sold by lot. An “enhanced hammer” deal, coded “105” or “109”, pays the seller the hammer plus part of the buyer’s premium; by 2016 Sotheby’s commission margin had fallen from about 21% to about 14% since 2009, which the former Christie’s executive Doug Woodham called “profoundly negative” for house economics.',c:'Ask the specialist whether a consignor deal is enhanced-hammer. It tells you how little margin the house has and how motivated it is.',v:'V',src:[['Artnet auction glossary, Nov 2025','https://news.artnet.com/market/art-auction-glossary-guide-2571010'],['WealthManagement: why houses give away millions','https://www.wealthmanagement.com/high-net-worth/deal-of-the-art-why-auction-houses-are-giving-away-millions']]},
{id:'guaranteed78',kind:'rule',st:['auction','lender'],img:'modi-nu-gauche',x:true,t:'78.3% of New York’s evening sales were guaranteed',b:'Bank of America and ArtTactic (22 April 2026) counted $1.93bn of guaranteed value against $0.53bn not guaranteed in the 2025 New York evening sales, a 78.3% share, up from 36% in 2016. Sotheby’s Lauder sale in November 2025 was 100% guaranteed, 19 of its 24 lots by third parties, and totalled $527.5m with Klimt’s Elisabeth Lederer at $236.4m; Christie’s double-header that week made $690m with 59 of 80 lots guaranteed. Sotheby’s wrote $902m of guarantees in 2007; at the end of 2008 the two houses were obliged to buy about $63m of guaranteed art. The often-quoted ≈$52m Sotheby’s loss of late 2008 is unverified.',c:'Before bidding, learn the guaranteed share of the sale. A guarantor can bid and is paid a fee if outbid.',v:'V',src:[['Bank of America Private Bank, spring 2026','https://www.privatebank.bankofamerica.com/articles/art-market-spring-update.html'],['Artnet: the Lauder sale by the numbers','https://news.artnet.com/market/sothebys-leonard-lauder-contemporary-by-the-numbers-2715607'],['Apollo on guarantees','https://apollo-magazine.com/auction-guarantees-irrevocable-bids-risks-christies-sothebys-phillips/']]},
{id:'guarantor',kind:'rule',st:['auction','lender'],t:'The guarantor’s upside',b:'A Brandeis working paper describes the economics: the upside above the guarantee is typically split between seller and guarantor after commission; at Christie’s the third-party guarantor “receives a share of the difference … even if he wins the auction”, whereas at Sotheby’s “the third-party guarantor cannot collect the financing fee if he wins”. Terms are private and vary by lot.',c:'A winning guarantor’s true cost may be below the published price. Published prices are not always what the buyer paid.',v:'S',src:[['Brandeis working paper 71 (PDF)','https://www.brandeis.edu/economics/RePEc/brd/doc/Brandeis_WP71.pdf']]},
{id:'cartel',kind:'case',year:2002,st:['auction'],t:'The commission cartel',b:'The Department of Justice found that Sotheby’s and Christie’s fixed sellers’ commissions from 1993 to 1999, during which US sellers paid at least $400m in commissions. Sotheby’s and its chief executive Diana Brooks pleaded guilty in October 2000; Sotheby’s paid a $45m fine; Christie’s escaped through the corporate leniency programme. Alfred Taubman was sentenced on 22 April 2002 to a year and a day and a $7.5m fine; Brooks got probation; Sir Anthony Tennant of Christie’s refused to travel and could not be extradited. A $512m civil settlement followed, $156m of it Taubman’s share, and the European Commission fined Sotheby’s nearly $20m in October 2002.',c:'The published rate card was collusive for years. Negotiate fees and guarantees rather than accept the schedule.',v:'V',story:'pricefixing',src:[['US Department of Justice, 2 May 2001','https://www.justice.gov/archive/opa/pr/2001/May/199at.htm'],['UPI, 23 Apr 2002','https://www.upi.com/Archives/2002/04/23/Sothebys-ex-chairman-gets-prison-term/6181019534400/'],['Irish Times on the EU fine','https://www.irishtimes.com/news/sotheby-s-fined-almost-20m-for-price-fixing-1.1103317']]},
// lending
{id:'lending',kind:'rule',st:['lender'],t:'$34–40bn of loans against art',b:'Deloitte Private and ArtTactic estimate outstanding art-secured loans at $29.2–34.1bn at the end of 2023 and $34–40bn going into 2026, from about $8bn in 2015. Loan-to-value is typically capped around 50% (30–50% in practice); rates run from 2–3% at private banks for the best borrowers to 9% or more at specialists. Sotheby’s Financial Services’ book is about $1.6bn, roughly double 2021; Bank of America Private Bank’s art-loan commitments grew 14% year on year in the first half of 2025. Lenders include Bank of America, Citi, JPMorgan, Morgan Stanley, Deutsche Bank, Athena, The Fine Art Group and Borro.',c:'Expect 50% of a conservative appraisal, a UCC-1 filing, and a lender’s consent to any sale.',v:'S',src:[['ArtTactic: Art & Finance Report 2025','https://arttactic.com/reports/deloitte-and-arttactic-or-art-and-finance-report-2025'],['The Art Newspaper, 8 Mar 2023','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2023/03/08/art-loans-spike-as-specialist-lenders-multiply'],['Masterworks: lending market size','https://www.masterworks.com/academy/posts/art-lending-market-size-and-growth-where-the-industry-stands-in-2026']]},
{id:'philbrick',kind:'case',year:2022,st:['lender','private','advisor'],t:'Philbrick sold more than 100% of the same pictures',b:'From 2016 to 2019 Inigo Philbrick sold fractional stakes in the same works, by Basquiat, Wool and Stingel among others, to several buyers at once, using forged contracts and pledging works he did not own. He fled to Vanuatu in October 2019, was arrested there on 11 June 2020, pleaded guilty in November 2021 and on 23 May 2022 was sentenced to 84 months with a forfeiture order of $86,672,790. His partner Robert Newland got 20 months. Philbrick was released in March 2024 and has spoken of a return to dealing; Orlando Whitfield’s book All That Glitters (2024) is the inside account.',c:'For any fractional or loan-backed deal, confirm title with the registered owner and run a lien search.',v:'V',story:'philbrick',src:[['US Department of Justice, 23 May 2022','https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/former-art-dealer-sentenced-7-years-86-million-fraud-scheme'],['Artnet, 2024','https://news.artnet.com/art-world-archives/disgraced-art-dealer-inigo-philbrick-is-out-of-prison-and-planning-a-comeback-2460533']]},
// museums
{id:'aamd',kind:'rule',st:['museum','estate'],t:'“Direct care”: when museums may sell',b:'The Association of Art Museum Directors’ pandemic resolutions, from 15 April 2020 to April 2022, let sale proceeds fund “direct care” and restricted-endowment income cover operations; Brooklyn and the Everson sold, and Baltimore cancelled a planned $65m sale of a Warhol, a Marden and a Still. The 2022 revised policy permanently permits proceeds for direct care, narrowed to conservation treatments and storage materials and excluding salaries and exhibitions.',c:'When a museum consigns, read its deaccession resolution and the AAMD-conforming use of proceeds.',v:'V',src:[['The Art Newspaper, 11 May 2022','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2022/05/11/us-museums-deaccessioning-what-next'],['ARTnews on the AAMD policy','https://www.artnews.com/art-news/news/aamd-amends-deaccessioning-policy-1234641312/']]},
{id:'kawamura',kind:'case',year:2025,st:['museum','auction'],t:'A corporate museum’s Monet',b:'The Kawamura Memorial DIC Museum of Art in Chiba, owned by DIC Corporation, closed on 31 March 2025 under pressure from the activist fund Oasis Management and consigned 80 works to Christie’s. Its Monet Nymphéas of 1907, estimated at $40–60m, hammered at $39m, $45.4m with fees, on 17 November 2025.',c:'A corporate-owned museum’s sale is a shareholder decision, not an AAMD process. Expect more of them.',v:'V',img:'monet-bassin',src:[['The Art Newspaper, 19 Sep 2025','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2025/09/19/christies-kawamura-memorial-dic-museum-art-auction-christies'],['The Art Newspaper, 18 Nov 2025','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2025/11/18/kicking-off-new-york-november-sales-christies-nets-healthy-5747m-from-double-header-20th-century-auction']]},
{id:'loans',kind:'rule',st:['museum','restitution'],t:'Loans can be recalled, and seized',b:'The Staechelin family trust’s Gauguin, Nafea faa ipoipo, hung at the Kunstmuseum Basel on long-term loan until its reported ≈$210m sale in 2015, when the loan ended and the picture left. Egon Schiele’s Portrait of Wally, lent by the Leopold collection to MoMA, was seized by the Manhattan District Attorney in January 1998 on a claim by the heirs of Lea Bondi Jaray; the Leopold Museum settled in July 2010 for $19m. Lenders rely on immunity-from-seizure laws (the US act of 1965; the UK’s 2007 act) that cover some claims and not others.',c:'Check the loan agreement’s recall terms and the immunity-from-seizure status before a loan crosses a border.',v:'S',img:'schiele-wally',src:[['Wikipedia: When Will You Marry?','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/When_Will_You_Marry%3F'],['Wikipedia: Portrait of Wally','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Wally']]},
{id:'sackler',kind:'rule',st:['museum','estate'],t:'Names come off the wall',b:'The Sackler name was removed by the Louvre (July 2019), Dia (2019), the Met (December 2021), the Serpentine (2021), the British Museum (March 2022), the Guggenheim and the National Gallery in London (May 2022), Tate (2022), Oxford’s Ashmolean and Bodleian (2023) and the V&A (2023); Harvard and the Smithsonian’s Arthur M. Sackler Gallery retained it.',c:'Donor-naming contracts now routinely include morals and renaming clauses. Ask to see them before a gift.',v:'S',src:[['Wikipedia: things named after the Sackler family','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_things_named_after_the_Sackler_family']]},
// primary market
{id:'rofr',kind:'rule',st:['gallery','collector','private'],t:'The invoice terms you did not read',b:'Typical primary invoices carry no resale for three to five years, a right of first refusal, no auction during the window, and “buy one, give one” to a museum. In 2022 a Cecily Brown painting, Faeriefeller, bought from Paula Cooper for $700,000 under a three-year right of first refusal, was flipped within four months through Lévy Gorvy; the buyer sued for more than $1.3m and the case settled within hours of publication. No US court has ruled on enforceability because every such suit has settled; Wildenstein v. Wallis upheld a limited right of first refusal, and an undisclosed restriction has been allowed to proceed as a breach of warranty of title. In the UK, Taylor Wessing argued in June 2024 that such clauses risk breaching the Competition Act 1998.',c:'Ask for the original gallery invoice. An undisclosed resale restriction can impair title.',v:'V',src:[['Artnet: the gray market','https://news.artnet.com/market/gray-market-huang-debernardi-paula-cooper-2061364'],['Art Law Podcast on resale restrictions','https://artlawpodcast.com/2024/10/08/enforceability-and-effectiveness-of-art-market-resale-restrictions/'],['Taylor Wessing, June 2024','https://www.taylorwessing.com/en/insights-and-events/insights/2024/06/artistic-control-or-market-manipulation']]},
{id:'discounts',kind:'rule',st:['gallery','studio'],t:'10, 15–20, and 50/50',b:'Ten per cent is the conventional collector discount, 15–20% for museums and institutions; the gallery–artist split is usually 50/50, and dealers often absorb discounts from their own share rather than the artist’s.',c:'A “museum price” is 15–20% off list. A first-time buyer rarely gets more than 10%.',v:'V',src:[['Artsy: how dealers discount','https://www.artsy.net/article/artsy-editorial-strategies-art-dealers-discount-artists-work']]},
{id:'flipping',kind:'case',year:2014,st:['gallery','collector','auction'],t:'The flip of 2013–15',b:'A 2011 Cooper Union thesis “rain painting” by Lucien Smith first sold for $10,000 and resold at Phillips in November 2013 for $389,000; his auction sales passed $4m in 2014. By 2015 prices had fallen sharply and Alberto Mugrabi called the work “practically unsellable”. Walter Robinson named the style “zombie formalism” in 2014. Smith died on 7 September 2026, aged 37.',c:'Auction records for painters under thirty reflect speculation more than demand. Look at the buy-in rate, not the record.',v:'V',src:[['Artnet obituary, Sep 2026','https://news.artnet.com/art-world/lucien-smith-obituary-2808360'],['Hyperallergic, Sep 2026','https://hyperallergic.com/artist-lucien-smith-fixture-of-nycs-downtown-scene-dies-at-37/']]},
// insurance & logistics
{id:'insurance',kind:'rule',st:['logistics','collector','fair'],t:'0.1–0.5% a year, nail to nail',b:'Annual fine-art premiums typically run 0.1–0.5% of agreed value; “nail to nail” exhibition cover, from the lender’s wall to the borrower’s and back, is priced per show, and one 256-painting Picasso exhibition in China in 2005 cost more than $12.1m to insure. On 24 May 2004 an arson at the Momart warehouse in Leyton destroyed more than 100 works, including the Chapmans’ Hell and Emin’s tent, many owned by Charles Saatchi and valued at £30–50m; negligence claims settled by mediation for “tens of millions of pounds”.',c:'Insist on an agreed-value policy and a signed condition report at each nail. Know whether your policy covers storage at a third party.',v:'V',src:[['Atlas Magazine: fine-art insurance','https://www.atlas-mag.net/en/articles/fine-arts-insurance'],['Wikipedia: Momart','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Momart']]},
{id:'cyber',kind:'case',year:2024,st:['data','auction'],t:'Christie’s, May 2024: passports in the wind',b:'RansomHub claimed the attack on Christie’s in May 2024; the house disclosed that clients’ names, dates of birth, addresses, nationalities and passport numbers were taken. The US class covered 45,798 people and a $990,000 settlement (S.D.N.Y. 1:24-cv-04221) won preliminary approval in February 2025, paying about $100 a claimant.',c:'Never e-mail passport scans to a house. Use its KYC portal, and ask how long it keeps them.',v:'V',src:[['ClassAction.org on the settlement','https://www.classaction.org/news/990k-christies-settlement-ends-data-breach-class-action-lawsuit'],['The Art Newspaper, 28 May 2024','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2024/05/28/hackers-claim-christies-cyberattack-and-threaten-to-release-client-data']]},
// restitution & antiquities
{id:'washington',kind:'rule',st:['restitution','auction','private'],t:'1933–45: the gap that voids a sale',b:'The 1998 Washington Principles were endorsed by 44 countries. On 5 March 2024, 21 further countries endorsed the State Department’s Best Practices, which treat sales under duress as Nazi-looted, call for public provenance disclosure and archive access, and recommend independent claims bodies; a survey of 47 countries found only seven had made major progress.',c:'For anything with a 1933–45 European gap, run lootedart.com and the Lost Art database before quoting.',v:'V',src:[['JTA, 8 Mar 2024','https://www.jta.org/2024/03/08/culture/21-countries-agree-to-updated-guidelines-on-nazi-looted-art-as-researchers-say-much-work-remains']]},
{id:'kandinsky',kind:'case',year:2026,st:['restitution','auction'],t:'Das bunte Leben: a restitution priced at $30m',b:'Emanuel and Hedwig Lewenstein bought Kandinsky’s Das bunte Leben in 1927 and deposited it at the Stedelijk in 1933; associates of the Nazi banker Alois Miedl took it and it was auctioned in October 1940. Bayerische Landesbank bought it in 1972 and lent it to the Lenbachhaus. The heirs sued in 2017; restitution was recommended in 2023 and honoured. Christie’s London offers it on 14 October 2026 with an expectation “in excess of $30m”; the heirs’ agent is Mondex.',c:'A restituted work’s provenance is now its selling point. Know who the claims agent is; it tells you how the deal will run.',v:'V',img:'kand-bunte',x:true,src:[['Artnet, 2026','https://news.artnet.com/market/kandinsky-colorful-life-restitution-auction-christies-2805522']]},
{id:'nahmad',kind:'case',year:2026,st:['restitution','company','auction'],img:'modi-blue',t:'The Nahmad Modigliani, eleven years on',b:'On 3 April 2026 Justice Joel M. Cohen of the New York Supreme Court granted summary judgment in Greason v. Nahmad: David Nahmad’s Panamanian company, International Art Center, must return Modigliani’s Seated Man with a Cane (1918) to the estate of the Paris dealer Oscar Stettiner, finding the 1996 provenance “contained false information”. Nahmad had bought it at Christie’s London in June 1996 for £2m, about $3.2m, and kept it in Switzerland; the Panama Papers exposed the ownership in 2016. On 16 June 2026 he was given 30 days to return the work, now valued around $30m; he has said he will appeal.',c:'A Christie’s or Sotheby’s sale record is not a title clearance, and an offshore company does not shield a work from a claim.',v:'V',src:[['NY Courts: Greason v. Nahmad, 2026 NY Slip Op 50461(U)','https://www.nycourts.gov/reporter/current/3dseries/2026/2026_50461.shtml'],['The Art Newspaper, 17 Jun 2026','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2026/06/17/david-nahmad-given-30-days-return-nazi-looted-modigliani']]},
{id:'steinhardt',kind:'case',year:2021,st:['restitution','collector'],t:'Steinhardt’s lifetime ban',b:'On 6 December 2021 Michael Steinhardt surrendered 180 antiquities valued at $70m, looted from 11 countries through 12 networks, and accepted the first lifetime ban on acquiring antiquities, with no charges. The Manhattan District Attorney’s Antiquities Trafficking Unit reported on 10 December 2025 a cumulative 18 convictions, more than 6,100 antiquities valued at $480m recovered and more than 5,780 returned to 32 countries; a further 657 objects worth about $14m went to India on 30 April 2026.',c:'Any antiquity without a documented pre-1970 export history is a seizure risk in New York.',v:'V',src:[['CNBC, 6 Dec 2021','https://www.cnbc.com/2021/12/06/hedge-fund-pioneer-michael-steinhardt-surrenders-stolen-antiquities-vance-says-.html'],['Manhattan DA','https://manhattanda.org/d-a-bragg-announces-return-of-43-antiquities-to-the-people-of-turkiye/']]},
{id:'met',kind:'rule',st:['restitution','museum'],t:'The Met’s Khmer returns and its new provenance desk',b:'In December 2023 the Metropolitan Museum deaccessioned 14 Khmer sculptures to Cambodia and 2 to Thailand linked to the dealer Douglas Latchford; it created a provenance initiative in 2023 and appointed Lucian Simmons Head of Provenance in March 2024 with a staff of 11; 2024 returns included a Sumerian sculpture to Iraq. The 1970 UNESCO Convention has 149 states parties; the US Cultural Property Implementation Act of 1983 implements only Articles 7(b) and 9 through renewable bilateral agreements recommended by the eleven-member CPAC.',c:'Latchford, Kapoor and Steinhardt in a provenance are red flags. Check whether the source country has a US agreement and whether the object type is on its designated list.',v:'V',src:[['The Met press release','https://www.metmuseum.org/press-releases/cambodia-transfer-2024-news'],['UNESCO 1970','https://www.unesco.org/en/fight-illicit-trafficking/about'],['AIA overview of CPAC (PDF)','https://www.archaeological.org/pdfs/sitepreservation/CPAC_OverviewAIA.pdf']]},
{id:'gilgamesh',kind:'case',year:2021,st:['restitution','auction'],t:'The Gilgamesh Dream Tablet',b:'Hobby Lobby bought the tablet at Christie’s in 2014 for $1.67m for the Museum of the Bible; the United States seized it in 2019 and forfeited it in August 2021, and it was returned to Iraq with about 17,000 other objects at a Smithsonian ceremony on 23 September 2021. Hobby Lobby had paid $3m in a 2017 Justice Department settlement over illegally imported tablets, and sued Christie’s over the tablet’s documentation.',c:'A dealer’s paperwork is no defence for antiquities. Require export licences, not letters.',v:'V',src:[['NPR, 21 Sep 2021','https://www.npr.org/2021/09/21/1039380004/gilgamesh-dream-tablet-hobby-lobby-iraq-return'],['Al Jazeera, 22 Sep 2021','https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2021/9/22/us-to-return-gilgamesh-tablet-17000-artifacts-taken-from-iraq']]},
{id:'gurlitt',kind:'case',year:2014,st:['restitution'],t:'The Gurlitt trove',b:'Some 1,280 works were found in Cornelius Gurlitt’s Munich flat in 2012, with more in Salzburg. Gurlitt died in May 2014 and the Kunstmuseum Bern accepted his bequest that November. Bern’s tally in December 2021, of about 1,600 works: 9 definitively looted and restituted, 29 renounced as suspected loot, 1,089 with no direct evidence of looting, 28 verified clean for 1933–45; 14 works have been restituted in all, the last Spitzweg’s Das Klavierspiel in January 2021. The research project ended in 2017 with more than 600 works in a grey zone.',c:'An artist’s name says nothing about a work’s 1933–45 history. Grey zones stay grey for decades.',v:'V',src:[['The Art Newspaper, 17 Dec 2021','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2021/12/17/kunstmuseum-bern-gurlitt-trove-nazi-loot'],['Hyperallergic on the restitutions','https://hyperallergic.com/nazi-looted-artworks-gurlitt-trove-restituted/']]},
// fakes & attribution
{id:'knoedler',kind:'case',year:2016,st:['fakes','gallery'],t:'Knoedler: $80m of pictures painted in Queens',b:'Glafira Rosales supplied more than 60 fake Abstract Expressionist works, painted by Pei-Shen Qian in Queens, that two Manhattan galleries sold for more than $80m; she received about $33.2m. Knoedler closed in November 2011. Rosales pleaded guilty on 16 September 2013; the De Sole case over a fake Rothko bought in 2004 for about $8.3m settled mid-trial in February 2016, after a forensic accountant testified the gallery would have lost $3.2m without the Rosales works. Rosales was sentenced to time served in January 2017 with an $81m restitution order; Qian is in China and José Carlos Bergantiños Díaz avoided extradition from Spain.',c:'An anonymous “private collector” is not provenance. Demand a chain of custody and independent technical analysis.',v:'V',story:'knoedler',src:[['US Department of Justice, 2013','https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/art-dealer-pleads-guilty-manhattanfederal-court-80-million-fake-art-scammoney'],['The Art Newspaper, 1 Mar 2016','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2016/03/01/parties-settle-in-knoedler-case-as-court-hears-fakes-helped-keep-business-afloat']]},
{id:'beltracchi',kind:'case',year:2011,st:['fakes','auction'],t:'Beltracchi and the collection that never existed',b:'On 27 October 2011 a Cologne court sentenced Wolfgang Beltracchi to six years and his wife Helene to four for 14 forgeries sold with an invented provenance, the “Jägers collection”, prosecutors estimating about €16m in profit; the fourteen would have been worth about €34m if genuine. A Campendonk, Rotes Bild mit Pferden, sold at Lempertz in November 2006 for €2.88m and was exposed by titanium white pigment that did not exist in 1914. He was released in January 2015 and claims to have faked about 50 artists; police identified 58 suspected forgeries.',c:'A romantic provenance story plus a gap in the catalogue raisonné calls for pigment analysis before purchase.',v:'S',src:[['The Art Newspaper, Jul 2011','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2011/07/01/four-to-go-on-trial-in-german-werner-jager-forgery-case'],['Wikipedia: Wolfgang Beltracchi','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolfgang_Beltracchi']]},
{id:'boards',kind:'rule',st:['fakes'],t:'The boards that no longer answer',b:'The Andy Warhol Foundation announced in October 2011 that it would dissolve its authentication board after spending more than $7m defending the Simon-Whelan suit, which it won; the Haring and Basquiat foundations stopped authenticating in 2012 after legal challenges, and Thome v. Calder Foundation was dismissed. The status of the Lichtenstein and Pollock-Krasner boards is not confirmed here.',c:'With fewer boards, insist on a written authenticity warranty and a catalogue-raisonné entry, and budget for technical analysis.',v:'S',src:[['Center for Art Law on the Warhol board','https://itsartlaw.org/al-clippings/will-the-real-andy-warhol-please-stand-up-the-authentication-board-to-shut-down/'],['Antiques Trade Gazette, 2012','https://www.antiquestradegazette.com/news/2012/haring-and-basquiat-foundations-will-no-longer-authenticate-works/']]},
{id:'orlando',kind:'case',year:2023,st:['fakes','museum'],t:'A museum show is not authentication',b:'The FBI seized the Orlando Museum of Art’s “Basquiats” in June 2022. The Los Angeles auctioneer Michael Barzman pleaded guilty in April 2023 to making them and on 18 August 2023 received three years’ probation; the museum sued its fired director Aaron De Groft in August 2023; the owners’ $19.7m insurance claim is disputed on the ground that the works are inauthentic.',c:'Exhibition history is marketing. Authentication is a document.',v:'V',src:[['The Art Newspaper, 18 Aug 2023','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2023/08/18/auctioneer-basquiat-forgeries-orlando-museum-fbi-sentenced'],['Artnet, Jan 2025','https://news.artnet.com/art-world/insurers-fake-basquiat-orlando-museum-2600690']]},
{id:'salvator',kind:'case',year:2017,st:['fakes','private','company','freeport'],t:'Salvator Mundi: £45 to $450.3m to silence',b:'Sold at Sotheby’s London on 25 June 1958 for £45 as a Boltraffio; bought at a New Orleans estate sale in 2005 for a sum reported as $1,175 by some outlets and about $10,000 by others; restored and shown as a Leonardo at the National Gallery in 2011; sold privately through Sotheby’s in 2013 for $75–80m to Yves Bouvier, who resold it to Dmitry Rybolovlev for $127.5m; sold at Christie’s New York on 15 November 2017 for $450.3m after a nineteen-minute contest. The Wall Street Journal identified the buyer as Prince Badr bin Abdullah, for the Crown Prince; Saudi Arabia never confirmed it. The Prado’s 2021 catalogue listed it under the workshop; its whereabouts are unconfirmed.',c:'A $450m price is one unrepeatable data point for an unseen, contested work, not a comparable.',v:'V',story:'salvator',img:'leo-salvator',x:true,src:[['Artnet timeline','https://news.artnet.com/market/timeline-salvator-mundi-went-45-to-450-million-59-years-1150661'],['CNBC, 17 Nov 2017','https://www.cnbc.com/2017/11/17/da-vincis-salvador-mundi-went-from-under-200-to-a-record-breaking-450-million.html']]},
// advisors & intermediaries
{id:'bouvier',kind:'case',year:2024,st:['advisor','freeport','private'],t:'Bouvier: 38 works, $2bn, a $1bn margin',b:'Between 2003 and 2014 Yves Bouvier sold Dmitry Rybolovlev’s trusts 38 works for about $2bn with markups above $1bn, Salvator Mundi among them at $80m in and $127.5m out. Monaco arrested Bouvier in February 2015 and annulled the proceedings in December 2019; the parties reached a confidential global settlement in December 2023. On 30 January 2024 a New York jury found Sotheby’s not liable on all counts over four works. On 15 January 2026 a Paris investigating judge ordered Bouvier to trial for concealing stolen goods and money laundering over nearly 70 Picasso works taken from Catherine Hutin’s storage, €36m of them sold to Rybolovlev; no date is set.',c:'Get a written statement of whether your dealer acts as principal or agent, and disclosure of costs.',v:'V',story:'bouvier',img:'gauguin-tefare',x:true,src:[['The Art Newspaper, 30 Jan 2024','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2024/01/30/rybolovlev-sothebys-fraud-lawsuit-verdict'],['The Art Newspaper, 13 Mar 2026','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2026/03/13/yves-bouvier-to-stand-trial-in-paris']]},
{id:'schiff',kind:'case',year:2025,st:['advisor','collector'],t:'Lisa Schiff: the advisor who held the money',b:'From 2018 to May 2023 the New York advisor lied to at least twelve clients, an artist, an estate and a gallery about purchases and sales of about 55 works, using new clients’ money to cover old debts. She pleaded guilty to wire fraud on 17 October 2024 and on 19 March 2025 was sentenced to 30 months, with restitution of $9,147,789 and forfeiture of $6,408,538.',c:'Never let an advisor receive client funds. Pay the consignor directly against its invoice.',v:'V',src:[['US Department of Justice, 19 Mar 2025','https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/former-art-advisor-lisa-schiff-sentenced-30-months-prison-defrauding-clients'],['The Art Newspaper, 19 Mar 2025','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2025/03/19/art-adviser-lisa-schiff-sentenced-prison-fraud']]},
{id:'gulbenkian',kind:'case',year:2021,st:['advisor','private'],t:'Gulbenkian’s pumpkin',b:'Angela Gulbenkian, acting as agent for the seller of a Yayoi Kusama pumpkin sold for $1.375m, kept the $1.275m paid in October 2017; she also took £50,000 from her masseuse. Extradited from Portugal, she was sentenced at Southwark Crown Court on 29 July 2021 to three and a half years after pleading guilty to two counts of theft.',c:'Pay through escrow, never to an agent’s own account.',v:'V',src:[['Crown Prosecution Service','https://www.cps.gov.uk/london-south/news/art-dealer-and-socialite-jailed-over-theft-linked-1375m-pumpkin-sculpture'],['The Art Newspaper, 30 Jul 2021','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2021/07/30/former-dealer-angela-gulbenkian-is-sentenced-to-three-and-a-half-years-for-defrauding-art-clients']]},
{id:'sammons',kind:'case',year:2019,st:['advisor','lender'],t:'Sammons and Chowaiki: consigned, then pledged',b:'Timothy Sammons, a former Sotheby’s specialist turned agent, was sentenced in New York on 1 August 2019 to four to twelve years for defrauding clients of up to $30m between 2010 and 2015, selling or pledging consigned works including a Picasso Buste de femme, a Chagall and a Signac. Ezra Chowaiki, president of a Park Avenue gallery, pleaded guilty in May 2018 and on 27 September 2018 received 18 months, forfeiting interests in more than 20 works. Larry Salander, in 2010, owed about $120m.',c:'Consigned works get re-pledged. Check title and liens on anything an agent offers.',v:'V',src:[['The Art Newspaper, 1 Aug 2019','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2019/08/01/timothy-sammons-very-remorseful-as-he-is-jailed-for-defrauding-clients-of-up-to-dollar30m'],['US Department of Justice, Sep 2018','https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/president-park-avenue-art-gallery-sentenced-18-months-prison-defrauding-art-dealers-and']]},
{id:'wildenstein',kind:'case',year:2024,st:['estate','company','tax'],t:'Wildenstein: the trusts, retried',b:'The heirs of Daniel Wildenstein (d. 2001) were accused of hiding an estate in offshore trusts, including paintings valued at €1bn. Acquitted in 2017 and 2018, they were retried after the Cour de cassation ordered it in January 2021. On 5 March 2024 the Paris Court of Appeal convicted Guy Wildenstein: four years, two suspended, and a €1m fine; Alec Jr two years suspended; two asset managers fined €187,500 each. The final cassation outcome and the tax claimed are not confirmed here.',c:'Family-dealer provenance can carry trust and estate-tax encumbrances. Diligence the seller’s structure, not only the picture.',v:'S',story:'wildenstein',src:[['The Art Newspaper, 5 Mar 2024','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2024/03/05/paris-court-finds-billionaire-dealer-guy-wildenstein-guilty-of-tax-fraud'],['The Art Newspaper, 7 Jan 2021','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2021/01/07/french-high-court-orders-retrial-of-wildenstein-art-dynasty-tax-fraud-case']]},
{id:'boone',kind:'case',year:2019,st:['gallery','tax'],t:'Mary Boone’s books',b:'On 14 February 2019 the dealer Mary Boone was sentenced to 30 months for filing false tax returns for 2009–2011: a tax loss above $3m, restitution of $3,097,160, $793,003 of apartment remodelling written off as business expense and more than $560,000 withdrawn in cash. She surrendered in May 2019 and was released after about 13 months.',c:'Gallery books that mix personal and business spending are a counterparty-solvency signal. Ask for audited accounts before a large consignment.',v:'V',src:[['US Department of Justice, 14 Feb 2019','https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/manhattan-art-gallery-owner-mary-boone-sentenced-30-months-prison-filing-false-tax'],['Artnet','https://news.artnet.com/art-world/mary-boone-is-sentenced-1464904']]},
{id:'panama',kind:'case',year:2016,st:['company','estate','auction'],t:'Panama Papers: the Ganz sale had a prior buyer',b:'The 2016 leak showed that Christie’s November 1997 Ganz sale, which totalled $206.5m, had been preceded six months earlier by a $168m sale of the works through Spink & Son to Simsbury International, controlled by Joseph Lewis, then Christie’s largest shareholder, under a profit-share above $168m. The same leak exposed Nahmad’s Panamanian holding of the Stettiner Modigliani and an offshore company Rybolovlev used in his divorce.',c:'Check for hidden economic interests behind estate-sale totals and provenance companies.',v:'V',src:[['The Art Newspaper, 8 Apr 2016','https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2016/04/08/panama-papers-expose-art-worlds-offshore-secrets']]},
{id:'advisors2025',kind:'case',year:2025,st:['advisor','gallery'],t:'2025: an advisory firm dissolves, a gallery closes',b:'Guggenheim Asher Associates, a 37-year-old advisory firm, dissolved in 2025 with mutual fraud claims pending in the New York Supreme Court, Barbara Guggenheim alleging more than $20m siphoned; the Blum gallery closed abruptly in the summer; the Stern heirs sued the Met and the Goulandris Foundation in October 2025 over a Van Gogh.',c:'Check the finances and key-person risk of advisors and galleries before you consign or pay.',v:'S',src:[['Artnet: 8 controversies of 2025','https://news.artnet.com/art-world/8-controversies-that-rocked-the-art-world-in-2025-2728947']]},
{id:'wally',kind:'case',year:2010,st:['museum','restitution','border'],t:'Wally: a loan seized at the museum door',b:'Schiele’s Portrait of Wally was lent by Rudolf Leopold’s collection to the Museum of Modern Art in 1997 and seized in January 1998 by the Manhattan District Attorney and then by US Customs on a claim by the heirs of the dealer Lea Bondi Jaray, who had been forced to give it up in 1939. After twelve years of litigation the Leopold Museum paid the estate $19m in July 2010 and kept the picture, with a permanent label telling its story.',c:'A loan crossing a border is exposed to that border’s courts. Check the immunity-from-seizure grant before shipping.',v:'S',img:'schiele-wally',x:true,src:[['Wikipedia: Portrait of Wally','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Wally']]}
];

/* ten checks before any deal */
const CHECKS=[
['Identify the ultimate beneficial owner and screen OFAC, UK and EU lists with 50% look-through.','rotenberg'],
['Aggregate linked transactions: €10,000 or more in the EU or UK triggers due diligence, and in the UK the counterparty’s HMRC number.','amld5'],
['For a freeport-held work, obtain the inventory record and the declared owner; Geneva records before 2016 may not exist.','geneva'],
['Read that sale’s own symbol key (○ or °, ♦ or ⋑, ¤ or Δ) and ask whether an irrevocable bid has raised the reserve.','symbols'],
['Confirm the authenticity guarantee is five years, original-buyer-only and non-transferable before relying on it in a resale.','warranty'],
['Ask for the original gallery invoice to surface resale restrictions, rights of first refusal and buy-one-give-one obligations.','rofr'],
['Match the shipping documents to the real delivery address, and never accept a resale certificate for a personal purchase.','nyag'],
['Classify the object for customs (9701–9705, 9706 or 9403) and price in import VAT and ARR for the jurisdiction.','tariffs'],
['For a 1933–45 European gap or an undocumented antiquity, run Lost Art and lootedart.com and check the Manhattan DA and museum return lists.','washington'],
['If financing is involved, budget for about 50% loan-to-value, a UCC-1 search and the lender’s consent to sell.','lending']
];

